to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/17/17588b058b1e1fe934e06679836234f0ef35fe4484a558fcc3ec3b2aafbd2620.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/61/613ba027e9564812a8e050efd69fd98fff3f614f7d2ec69eeb787c628ccabd12.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/87/87579b882ca6d8c92d4a7adfed575873fd07c36ab20251722c2cc8fc0f0328e8.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/9f/9fc2431aa874c68b23b6640da987b2f3bc37e2261936f7e1656b04bc3fc0030d.png)\n\nand $\\boldsymbol{f}'$ at ![](images/b8/b83c8f9cb4130103d63821f0d0de50ec0628138dcc8faaf16e8cf9fc27f1a994.png) is\n\n![](images/08/0851481c21c553b6e0e06a795815b1845e96e0a14f805e440529d0f4d6f85323.png)" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186198053 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/28/286bec34ba72c733264e8223150d56566d0f61797cb221bed0af0323c3e190c9.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/33/33b42ebfc6c3f32ef92212f61a138080543e2e2981088f7b5760b23f7ac31c6c.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/35/354f034a96ffe5bde885644792f50636dc8cbde6f00d65159b0bdefdd836fa09.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/90/900488a105afb5aa10e9113db36cd9f2e834eac46300d7c0a521a7100ae0d88f.svg)\n\nand $\\boldsymbol{f}'$ at ![](images/ed/ed7c974c8fca89e98f542231572e558caf867b200843d2b222ccda4c68a1c33b.svg) is\n\n![](images/ab/abd152888f711ecd43a47e31269e40985178782591546e9d96912fd4c230afa0.svg)" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198054 1071 "For the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ defined as\n\n![](images/9f/9fb4fd5dfb788d65cc7ea3fd67bce06097c24e03454c3bd0c2971a4c24789f98.png)\n\nthe total derivative $\\boldsymbol f'$ is given by \n\n![](images/e5/e5100618308bb9c2d07f0a022c18da152d88f30a3c7f3e0e1e173e9d743ff278.png)\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186198054 1071 "For the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ defined as\n\n![](images/bb/bbb9192192b4d73f4993b3131a2b0bb1d0a0cddb77b754940d1b7684bfb08336.svg)\n\nthe total derivative $\\boldsymbol f'$ is given by \n\n![](images/2c/2cf231879630af578e33b201e5be0f65d019da9c49da7789f5921c05a8249c09.svg)\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198054 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/49/49c118b037a1589c6feb2c96afeaccb15fe905663393800638baff8cef327a3a.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/a5/a5ed3766e8ed6a2a5fd249078e244dfab19b1c20d55548c27a7053bd0130c278.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/23/23d7804912a10685756bb4ec89d12e3f6659b435ebe4d22c436684fb6eef9df8.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{y}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/ea/eae5dd91db0cc17bec3af0a4781c7a58e8c65edb6c4139642f9c806dfc9c2473.svg)\nThus, the missing entries are $y,\\:$ $0,\\:$ and $\\ln{x}.$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198054 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/f1/f1c18d26fb7b6f1969bcda14d71ac18429466b4be8847097f227cdc537fdc8d9.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/4b/4b752e33d4ca3154590a5dc16ec3addd1d45a9618c92283903ff33d3818b8266.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/d1/d10e552c42ff8fe0df517b6297582bd5eeefdf29896a51005c79bd399a363c13.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{y}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/8c/8c2cef2ba5322cc545860caa4d2f503f5f48a3374cce7c0f1e910a56f0357a64.png)\nThus, the missing entries are $y,\\:$ $0,\\:$ and $\\ln{x}.$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186198055 1071 "Consider the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n![](images/7c/7c033fac25d53675fc47eaf5184d33f95c6e3524d82a4c547fb900e13c5205f7.svg)\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at ![](images/9c/9c89326030e3216291d4c0fbd715cdd634a567b6dae3f0d26620d19ee5f470ea.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/62/62ad44fc4734bc7006e9cc72a3e142d2df1d932f3fcdf1c0c6c125e8bd19d626.svg)\n\nfind the missing values." 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198055 1071 "Consider the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n![](images/d1/d18d3d4b6ce4dc901608a0c8b91b245aa8922ad950d7b664b4de8320aa3c0818.png)\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at ![](images/62/62277cb0f04f6106a3039c916f28fbdf561bfdba6e08d576f1ed906b973ad90d.png) is given by\n\n![](images/d2/d2fcbb3375b9efa65559ba261581cf349dc801b3d84be5b465c36a7f4027c556.png)\n\nfind the missing values." 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186198055 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/82/826e5972b8b35e46f759dfb8f465316db48e36b12ef37d73b7bce6931f65a3d9.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/85/85c62ebacb5d1aaf052165a76d44b42dc83a2918020320326fd4bbe8cc4da14c.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/93/93faf7b5ccbadf7600ec47c7e63cac088211130f0f30109fbc71f2d7081139b9.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})=3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/65/65e55a996288ffb34ef5b44b9b49dc7adaf681d379625733e7e74f8363f3f36f.svg)\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at ![](images/6a/6a0a72041eadc608c5f2ef7217a5e627115cee3bce332751a5cb46acd9dc0c24.svg) we have\n\n![](images/ee/ee17f93ff906242db59f3ef934b9501e7335a46ba5710e3fd89645e83be5ba34.svg)\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](images/42/42b3574762b1d9b14d27f8ba29b5c89bca66ec03724fbec7557c7bf6e5524972.svg)" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198055 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/e7/e79145a13ada389ac337a69f8c48952f53b70d5caf48d950b305275ed8773b44.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/a4/a4de8e3eca77241030facac81ea44493400e00e34d1c7dde9ee9b25eab8441b0.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/5e/5ee75b93739a5018486c6b65a5e5c996f95b7f8ea6fdf6bd68b5727742e770fa.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})=3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/a8/a8fd9120b29337c656c9b715bfa38dbecc7755e50877fad9719de54ed248a8ed.png)\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at ![](images/44/445be7536a39ee9c8d90f4cee571bf46acb5c995a9b53547f1aa582bd351592a.png) we have\n\n![](images/44/44faf9caacb8100249410ce233429c030fc52656be35c31d22f423397288da03.png)\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](images/58/5824c66708813f59e2920287d35500ea68356ead4a4c3673d8807a0b81ca1abe.png)" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186198056 1071 "Consider the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ defined as\n\n![](images/27/27a38861d0d2b0a8be98704202927c7a0dc0c638d11f25733b5a1e24faa18de2.png)\n\nIf the linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol f$ at ![](images/fa/fafc23364eb261c2d42baebfee5aec21491567c7c8d7cdbeb5c1f5c6c9d30ae8.png) is given by\n\n![](images/b8/b86418e6cd8fdf247d775882927c1a9d7a6e0cd392f8bd79b93e6b58f267fef7.png)\n\nwhat are the missing values?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186198056 1071 "Consider the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ defined as\n\n![](images/f8/f8eb8b775c1645d73acd7313f54bdff4cdb55830ac6ebea2165c9263321ffb9e.svg)\n\nIf the linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol f$ at ![](images/18/18b82def6f36d90157e99ef72f14b857cd1e0c3007e684c660c31fd61f805040.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/91/91e5867a6be8302649edc9c2e1e4c4642c60853c7c3ad9bb2d25f9c649b5889d.svg)\n\nwhat are the missing values?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198056 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/43/43ffd01ab7a6d78ea0343953b985d9c4165ee76085f345bcd500c053550e3ec9.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/51/51191f4e8b1417de7b391a71d68c7c5917ed7083cc3b7a630662d6a0912b969c.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/06/066be34bb8bd7f585bc3d2666007e189f6c82f2e3b7f7a699928066825fd765d.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})={x}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{1},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/86/869b2f5de537961c4c83523ac6fcfd96329fc062e34509d88b8516b7a2259b84.svg)\n\nEvaluating $\\boldsymbol{f}$ and its derivative at ![](images/b3/b35d7585ed24aea694c3a31e69c1406744986db30cddf8842a5bae8d6d745e4b.svg) we have\n\n![](images/9f/9f3dbb19be577a74b8f19fd1eb5d42910fd0e46637388a3daab80c17d6edd833.svg)\n\nThe linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}\\left(\\mathbf x \\right) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](images/5d/5d526b2485b53b029bc119e66e6a385d0ba228ecca1ffd0c58e7934e83a97a7b.svg)" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198056 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/eb/eb248ccd80e8a10edab9d44c4cb11841aad35f6d35a778ff828607a312fcad35.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/47/47d512d278eaa912a112e96406e44d8b9a464984487e0ab9ff253a233441c2e1.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/4b/4bb0fb226d008d5efe2e7a04a444a2313b2acf80f71589a2aa0a17e40246c03b.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})={x}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{1},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/9e/9eb84e24979469e9b1618f0a20f030963741a513292699ed4c4d342cd9d8e00d.png)\n\nEvaluating $\\boldsymbol{f}$ and its derivative at ![](images/22/22f4d34b8ec830740b285e1c31954b1e382264c4f366d3ea426ecadd3fd5f5bb.png) we have\n\n![](images/65/651b14a10334bdfc073b86359ce306c1b4ba25b9390f345b609978e34cc08cad.png)\n\nThe linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}\\left(\\mathbf x \\right) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](images/a0/a00401d8a9246532f363238c37646a6640a904db7f53d165d4f29c07e0a81a01.png)" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186198411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427518 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198497 1050 840.0 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198633 1071 "For the transformation $f: R^{3} → R^{2}$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\ln (xyz) \\\\ x^{2}yz\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$,\n\nthe total derivative $f^{′}$ is given by\n\n$f^{′}(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & A A & \\frac{1}{z} \\\\ A A & x^{2}z & A A\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186198633 1071 "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz) \\\\ {x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & \\frac{1}{z} \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & {x}^{2}z & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198633 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{y},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)=2xyz,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)={x}^{2}z,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)={x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{1}{z} \\\\ 2xyz & {x}^{2}z & {x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198653 1071 "Consider the function $f: R^{2} → R$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = e^{x} + y, x = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$z - ∗ = \\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186198653 1071 "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+y,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186198653 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}+y)={e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-2=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n2+1+1=4.\n$$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186199487 1064 "We've spent some time studying so-called **plane transformations** of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll generalize our understanding of derivatives to transformations (or functions) of the form \n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.\n$$\n\nTo motivate the discussion, we start by considering functions of one variable. \n\nRecall that a function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is **differentiable** at a point $x = a$ if there exists a number $f'(a)$, called the **derivative** of $f$ at $a$, such that\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nWe can restate this definition by applying basic algebraic properties of limits.\n\nSince the left-hand side, $f'(a)$, is just a constant, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} f'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a},\n$$\n\nusing the fact that the limit of a constant is the constant itself.\n\nBringing all terms to one side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} - f'(a) \\right) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rearranged as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a) - f'(a)(x - a)}{x - a} \\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis final expression tells us that the difference between $f(x)$ and its *linear approximation* $f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)$ becomes negligible relative to $x - a$ as $x$ approaches $a$.\n\nIn other words, not only does $f(x)$ become close to $f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)$ near $a$, but the error between them shrinks *faster than* the distance $|x - a|$ itself.\n\nLet's see how we can generalize this definition to vector-valued functions." 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186199487 1064 "We've spent some time studying so-called **plane transformations** of the form\n\n$$\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2.$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll generalize our understanding of derivatives to transformations (or functions) of the form\n\n$$\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.$$\n\nTo motivate the discussion, we start by considering functions of one variable.\n\nRecall that a function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is **differentiable** at a point $x = a$ if there exists a number $f'(a)$, called the **derivative** of $f$ at $a$, such that\n\n$$f'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a}.$$\n\nWe can restate this definition by applying basic algebraic properties of limits.\n\nSince the left-hand side, $f'(a)$, is just a constant, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\\lim_{x \\to a} f'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a},$$\n\nusing the fact that the limit of a constant is the constant itself.\n\nBringing all terms to one side, we obtain\n\n$$\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} - f'(a) \\right) = 0,$$\n\nwhich can be rearranged as\n\n$$\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a) - f'(a)(x - a)}{x - a} \\right) = 0.$$\n\nThis final expression tells us that the difference between $f(x)$ and its *linear approximation* $f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)$ becomes negligible relative to $x - a$ as $x$ approaches $a$.\n\nIn other words, not only does $f(x)$ become close to $f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)$ near $a$, but the error between them shrinks *faster than* the distance $|x - a|$ itself.\n\nLet's see how we can generalize this definition to vector-valued functions." 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186199488 1064 "The equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nLet's express this equation in terms of the total derivative.\n\nWriting our multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^2 \\to \\mathbb R$ as $f\\left(\\mathbf x \\right),$ where ![](images/88/88f9f758bffba384670449973c5c89edae05c67268d2914a12801b21b60a319f.svg) the total derivative of $f$ is given by\n\n![](images/56/56dc34676135e9c35b9e08cff732715c5d878d4a19f4fee6625384460ed6ee5f.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/ca/ca5b0074c216a3235f015d7b96dece9ffc6fc50eecbdd0b5e97dfbd89eb446fa.svg) Then, we can re-write the equation of the tangent plane as a matrix equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-{z}_{0} & =(x-{x}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) \\\\ z-{z}_{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-{x}_{0} \\\\ y-{y}_{0}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z-f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ z-f(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(a) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (x-a) \\\\ z-f(a) & ={f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane to $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a},$ in matrix form, is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186199488 1064 "The equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ is given by\n\n$$z - z_0 = (x-x_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).$$\n\nLet's express this equation in terms of the total derivative.\n\nWriting our multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^2 \\to \\mathbb R$ as $f\\left(\\mathbf x \\right),$ where $\\mathbf{x} = [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ the total derivative of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf x) = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf{a} = [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, we can re-write the equation of the tangent plane as a matrix equation as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}z-{z}_{0} & =(x-{x}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) \\\\ z-{z}_{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-{x}_{0} \\\\ y-{y}_{0}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z-f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ z-f(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(a) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (x-a) \\\\ z-f(a) & ={f}^{'}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane to $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a},$ in matrix form, is given by\n\n$$z - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).$$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186199489 1064 "If $f:\\mathbb R \\to \\mathbb R$ is differentiable at $x=a,$ then for points close to $x=a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx f(a) + f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe have a similar result for transformations of the form $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.$\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R^m$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ then for points close to $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf x) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this approximation for $\\boldsymbol f$ is affine. Thus, if a function is differentiable at a point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a,$ it is **locally affine** in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ \n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186199489 1064 "If $f:\\mathbb R \\to \\mathbb R$ is differentiable at $x=a,$ then for points close to $x=a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\n$$f(x) \\approx f(a) + f'(a)(x-a).$$\n\nWe have a similar result for transformations of the form $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.$\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R^m$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ then for points close to $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\n$$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf x) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).$$\n\nNotice that this approximation for $\\boldsymbol f$ is affine. Thus, if a function is differentiable at a point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a,$ it is **locally affine** in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186199495 1064 "Suppose that a transformation (or function) $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\\boldsymbol f:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions of $n$ variables. We say that $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is **differentiable** at a point $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ if there exists an $m \\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})$, called the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{a}$, such that\n\n$$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{a}} \\frac{ \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}) - \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) - \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) }{ ||\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}|| } = \\mathbf{0}.$$\n\nIn other words, near $\\mathbf{a}$, the function $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is well-approximated by the linear map\n\n$$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}),$$\n\nand the error between $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ and this linear approximation becomes negligible compared to the distance $\\|\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}\\|$ as $\\mathbf{x}$ approaches $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf x) = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We sometimes refer to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ simply as **the derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f(\\mathbf x).$\n- For scalar-valued functions of the form $f: \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R,$ the total derivative is the *transpose* of the gradient vector: $$\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a) = \\left(\\nabla f \\right)^T(\\mathbf a)$$\n- For vector-valued functions $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of a single parameter $t,$ the total derivative at $t=a$ is the usual column vector containing the partial derivatives of the components of $f{:}$ $$\\boldsymbol{f}'(a) = \\left.[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{n}}{∂t}\\end{aligned}]\\right._{t = a }$$\n\n- For functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: $$\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right) = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2)}$$ Similarly, for functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: $$\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right) = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2,f_3)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2,x_3)}$$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186199495 1064 "Suppose that a transformation (or function) $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/cd/cd9132d6a0e934349ae58a0718e6320cba3dd8ae84de7df2e3fb98b391256b57.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions of $n$ variables. \nWe say that $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is **differentiable** at a point $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ if there exists an $m \\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})$, called the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{a}$, such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{a}}\n\\frac{\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}) - \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) - \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n}{\n||\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}||\n}\n= \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, near $\\mathbf{a}$, the function $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is well-approximated by the linear map\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}),\n$$\n\nand the error between $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ and this linear approximation becomes negligible compared to the distance $\\|\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}\\|$ as $\\mathbf{x}$ approaches $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/6b/6bd7bcfb4e48c1833ff57c614bb18f469ca0a17e8a6e29d730b9e940c2f58984.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We sometimes refer to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ simply as **the derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f(\\mathbf x).$\n\n- For scalar-valued functions of the form $f: \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R,$ the total derivative is the *transpose* of the gradient vector:\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a) = \\left(\\nabla f \\right)^T(\\mathbf a)\n$$\n\n- For vector-valued functions $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of a single parameter $t,$ the total derivative at $t=a$ is the usual column vector containing the partial derivatives of the components of $f{:}$\n![](images/27/27651b3ca026cad63ee6cbbd88162d3ce85b9afc642986daf0ae76c6519bdc38.svg)\n\n- For functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: \n\n$$\n\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right)  = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2)}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: \n\n$$\n\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right)  = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2,f_3)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2,x_3)}\n$$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186401412 1071 "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xyz} \\\\ x+yz\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186401412 1071 "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xyz} \\\\ x+yz\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?" 13194139534605 17592186045417 false] [17592186427507 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427507 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427507 1083 #uuid "43e47057-a760-57db-a3bf-60ce0300791b" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427508 1080 "${x}^{2}y,\\,$ $\\frac{1}{z},\\,$ and ${x}^{2}y$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427508 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427508 1083 #uuid "52ae776e-08b3-54b7-a885-e9217261d1b0" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427509 1080 "${x}^{2}y,\\,$ $\\frac{1}{x},\\,$ and ${x}^{2}y$" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427509 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427509 1083 #uuid "595978fe-cf25-5127-9b94-47cb53657dd6" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427510 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & {e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427510 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427510 1083 #uuid "87f84105-a6a8-5b4c-a4a3-205cfc0c3ca1" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427511 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x} & x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427511 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534605 17592186045417 true] [17592186427511 1083 #uuid "9d3b